{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "The Fairmont, Morgantown & Pittsburgh Railroad - the 'Sheepskin Line' - was Morgantown, West Virginia's main passenger railroad. Completed to Morgantown in 1886 and on to the Pennsylvania state line 12 April 1894, it ran 56 miles from Palatine Junction at Fairmont, up the east bank of the Monongahela through Morgantown and Point Marion, to Uniontown, Pennsylvania. The Baltimore & Ohio began operating it in March 1915 and made it the FM&P Branch (later Subdivision) of its Pittsburgh Division. Passenger service ended 25 September 1953; the last passenger train through Morgantown, B&O No. 66, ran 21 September 1953. The feature is split at Morgantown, and again at 1915, so that each half carries the color of the company that actually ran its trains in each era (Rule 4). Most of the West Virginia half is now the Mon River Trail and the Caperton Trail.",
  "sources": [
   "https://abandonedonline.net/location/fairmont-morgantown-pittsburgh-railroad/",
   "https://montrails.org/rail-trail-history/",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Trains/ICC_valuations/Fairmont,_Morgantown_and_Pittsburg_Railroad",
   "https://www.wvencyclopedia.org/entries/311"
  ],
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "the year through passenger trains first ran Morgantown - Uniontown (1894 is the opening of the Morgantown - state line portion)",
   "train frequencies in every era - all headways here are estimates",
   "whether the State Line Railroad, which carried the route from the state line into Uniontown, ran its own passenger trains before the FM&P/B&O through service"
  ]
 },
